  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Baou de Saint-Jeannet is one of the most recognisable summits inland from Nice: a rocky plateau rising to 782 metres (the high point given by the town) that towers over the village of the same name. The climb alternates between a scrubland path, stretches between pines and a few rocky sections, before opening onto a vast summit plateau with an orientation table and a 360° panorama over the French Riviera, from Cap d'Antibes to the first foothills of the Préalpes.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The marked trail is manageable for most regular hikers. The town of Saint-Jeannet gives 5.94 km, about 2h50 of walking, 403 m of elevation gain and 388 m of descent (medium difficulty), between 429 m at the start and 782 m at the top, with a return that isn't necessarily by the same path; it recommends this summit as an easy outing in spring or autumn and describes an orientation table that helps pick out landmarks on the coast and neighbouring peaks. Loop variants exist, for example via the Baous de la Gaude. The town publishes no rule on dogs: to be safe, keep your dog on a leash on busy and rocky stretches. A collaborative hiking sheet (Visorando) for the neighbouring Baou de la Gaude circuit notes there is no water point on the way, only in the village, and possible herds on the trails of this massif. The site is very busy at weekends: it's best to set off early, especially in summer when the heat can be intense on the exposed, shadeless sections.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: bring plenty of water for your dog — water points are scarce along the route, and the summit plateau is very exposed to the sun.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The hilltop village of Saint-Jeannet, at the foot of the Baou, is worth a detour with its typical lanes and views over the Var valley. La Gaude, a neighbouring village, offers other hiking routes on its own Baou.</p>
    </div>

    <aside>
      <div class="sidebar-card">
        <h3>Worth checking on-site</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><svg><use href="#i-alert"/></svg> Access to forested areas is restricted when wildfire risk is high (mid-June to mid-September) — check the departmental map before setting off.</li>
          <li><svg><use href="#i-alert"/></svg> The rocky passage (the "voie rouge") below the summit is manageable going up on good rock, but explicitly discouraged for down-climbing (according to hiking and climbing sites). Come back down via a different route rather than the same passage.</li>
          <li><svg><use href="#i-check"/></svg> Little shade on the summit plateau — avoid the hottest hours in summer.</li>
        </ul>
